# --- runtime (control-plane spec §1.2: tracked framework vs gitignored exhaust) ---
.workflow-runtime/
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# --- per-worktree operator state (Tier C) — never committed ---
STATE.md
STATE.journal.md
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/transcript-backups/

# NOTE: `.claude/settings.json` is deliberately NOT ignored — it is a COMMITTED BASELINE (the generic
# permission floor) so the safety denies are active from `git clone`, before init.sh/gen-config have run.
# Settings load at session START, so a generated-only file leaves the first session with no floor at all.
# gen-config then SPECIALIZES it per worktree; in a worktree whose merge policy differs from the baseline
# the file will show as modified — that is expected. Do NOT commit a stream's specialized copy over the
# baseline: it would push one stream's merge policy onto everyone. Personal overrides go in
# settings.local.json (ignored above; gen-config never touches it).

# --- the HOST OVERLAY (see README "Why the overlay is not tracked here") ---
# This repo ships the framework CORE + *.example.yaml templates. The concrete overlay is per-adopter:
# `init.sh` generates it from the examples. Keeping it untracked here means a consumer that vendors this
# repo via `git subtree` never has its own overlay clobbered by an upstream pull. In a HOST project these
# same files ARE tracked (Tier A) — that is the correct place for them.
config/project.yaml
config/streams.yaml
ownership.yaml
review-ledger.md
# Concrete charters are overlay too, for the same reason: a host's SA.md carries ITS merge gates and
# domain rules, which an upstream pull must never clobber. Core ships `*.template.md` + `_TEMPLATE.md`;
# `init.sh` renders the concrete copies.
#
# ⚠ In a HOST project the concrete overlay (this block AND the four files above) must be tracked (Tier A)
# — but THIS FILE travels into the host's `<prefix>/` on a subtree add, and the deepest .gitignore wins,
# so it ignores the host's own overlay. A host-root negation does NOT override it. The host must
# `git add -f` each overlay file once; after that they are tracked and ignore rules no longer apply.
# See use-cases/UC-05 step 6b — skipping it leaves the overlay absent from every fresh clone.
charters/*.md
!charters/README.md
!charters/_TEMPLATE.md
!charters/*.template.md
